Answer statfs of a sus_kstat path with the numbers it had when the rule was added

Upstream branches inside vfs_statfs() for an app that asks about a path with a sus_kstat rule and
answers from a kstatfs saved with the rule, so a bind mount or overlay on the path does not show
in f_blocks, f_type or f_flags. The module only rewrote the stat family.

The snapshot is taken with vfs_statfs() when the rule is added or replaced and kept by update, which
only re-targets the rule, so it holds what the path showed before the mount. A probe on vfs_statfs()
was tried first and never fires where LTO inlines it into its callers, which is all of 6.1 and
statfs on 6.12 and 6.18. The table entries of statfs, fstatfs and the AArch32 statfs, fstatfs,
statfs64 and fstatfs64 are wrapped instead, like the stat family. Gated like the stat layer on
KernelSU's umount decision for the caller.

For a caller the rules apply to, the wrapper answers the call itself: it resolves the argument
once, takes vfs_statfs() of it, swaps in the snapshot of that inode if a rule holds one and fills
the buffer in the layout of the entry, with the checks of fs/statfs.c (EINVAL for a wrong
statfs64 size, ESTALE retry, EOVERFLOW for a field the 32-bit layout cannot hold, EFAULT).
Patching up the buffer after the original needed a second lookup of a user string or descriptor
that another thread can swap in between, and then the real numbers of a hidden path reach the
caller. It also walked the path twice and copied it into a PATH_MAX buffer. Every other caller,
and every call while no rule exists, goes to the original.

